        <div class="jarvis-document">
            <section id="google-access"><h2>Google access and purpose</h2><p>Jarvis connects only after you complete Google’s consent flow for the account involved. Account identity information associates a grant with the correct account. A connection for one account does not authorize another account.</p><p>The separate Gmail reading connection requests read-only Gmail access and account identity. The reviewed reader searches and retrieves messages and threads using bounded plain-text content. It does not fetch attachments or HTML bodies.</p><p>The dedicated Google connection requests Gmail compose access, access to events on calendars you own, read-only calendar-list access, and identity permissions. At Google’s permission level, compose access includes managing drafts, including deleting drafts, and sending email; owned-event access includes reading, creating, changing, and deleting events. Always review Google’s current consent wording. The installed application may enable fewer actions than these permissions allow. See Google’s <a href="https://developers.google.com/workspace/gmail/api/auth/scopes">Gmail permissions</a> and <a href="https://developers.google.com/workspace/calendar/api/auth">Calendar permissions</a>.</p><p>Read access supplies message and calendar context for the work you request. When selective monitoring is enabled, message excerpts can be used to identify relevant follow-ups and create local findings or notifications. Sending email and changing the mailbox remain disabled in the reviewed installed version; available actions may change in future versions.</p></section>
            <section id="local-data"><h2>What is stored on your computer</h2><p>Jarvis stores client credentials, refresh grants, and authorization verifier material locally with protection tied to the Windows user account. Account and consent metadata are stored in a local SQLite database.</p><p>Local chat, task, and calendar records also persist in SQLite. Selective monitoring, when enabled, can retain source excerpts, derived findings, evidence, progress checkpoints, and local notifications. Not every database record is encrypted.</p><p>The reviewed Gmail reader caches search and thread content in memory, with up to 20 entries and a maximum five-minute expiry. That cache limit does not apply to persistent records created by other features.</p></section>
            <section id="ai-processing"><h2>AI providers and connected services</h2><p>Jarvis supports AI processing choices that can involve locally hosted or remote providers, depending on configuration. Content included in an AI request may be processed by the selected provider. Review your provider configuration and that provider’s terms before including sensitive information.</p><p>The reviewed selective Gmail classifier can submit bounded message excerpts to a locally hosted Ollama service. That specific classifier does not use a remote model provider. This is not a claim that every Jarvis feature processes all information locally.</p><p>Google processes requests to its own services under its applicable policies. This notice does not establish a universal no-sharing, no-logging, or no-training guarantee across every Jarvis feature or configured provider.</p></section>
            <section id="retention"><h2>Retention, revocation, and deletion</h2><p>Persistent local records can remain after a session ends. There is no verified single automatic retention period or uniform deletion control for all Google-derived records in the reviewed application.</p><p>You can revoke Jarvis access through <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">your Google Account connections</a>. Revocation removes ongoing provider access; it does not automatically delete previously saved local records or copies you retain in backups.</p><p>For help identifying and removing local Jarvis records, contact support. Do not include passwords, client secrets, access tokens, private message bodies, or other sensitive records in a support request.</p></section>
